    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor.jpg" alt="Charles V, Holy Roman Emperor" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V, Holy Roman Emperor</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Charles_V,_Holy_Roman_Emperor,_seated_(1500%E2%80%931558),_formerly_attributed_to_Titian_(Alte_Pinakothek,_Munich).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Attributed to Titian, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Grand Duchy grew from the Florentine Republic, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-medici">Medici family</a> had dominated in the fifteenth century. After the republic’s last revival was crushed by imperial and papal forces in the siege of 1529–1530, the Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alessandro-de-medici">Alessandro de’ Medici</a> hereditary Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> in 1532. When Alessandro was murdered by his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorenzino-de-medici">Lorenzino</a> in January 1537, Florentine leaders chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cosimo-i-de-medici">Cosimo de’ Medici</a>, a seventeen-year-old from a junior branch of the family. Cosimo I proved a determined ruler: he defeated republican exiles at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-montemurlo">Battle of Montemurlo</a> in August 1537, freed himself from imperial tutelage, and with Spanish backing waged war on the Republic of Siena, whose army was defeated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-marciano">Battle of Marciano</a> in 1554. Siena surrendered in 1555, and in 1557 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II of Spain</a> granted it to Cosimo as a fief, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> kept the coastal fortresses of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-of-the-presidi">Stato dei Presidi</a>. Cosimo now ruled most of Tuscany. On 27 August 1569 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-v">Pope Pius V</a> raised him to the new rank of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duke-of-tuscany">Grand Duke of Tuscany</a>, crowning him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in March 1570. The Emperor initially objected that only he could grant such a title, but in 1576 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximilian-ii-holy-roman-emperor">Maximilian II</a> recognised it for Cosimo’s son Francesco I, completing the creation of a sovereign state that would last for almost three centuries.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/florence"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/florence.jpg" alt="Florence" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Firenze_-_Piazzale_Michelangelo,_Firenze,_Italy_-_April_6,_2015_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Giorgio Galeotti, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> had been one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>’s great centres of banking and textile manufacture, and under the early grand dukes its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> and woollen industries remained important, though they declined in the seventeenth century as competition from northern Europe grew. Much of the countryside was worked under <em>mezzadria</em>, a sharecropping system in which peasant families farmed land owned by urban patricians, the Church and religious institutions, dividing the harvest with the landlord. The grand dukes promoted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livorno">Livorno</a> as the state’s port. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinando-i-de-medici">Ferdinando I</a>’s laws of 1591 and 1593, known as the <em>Livornine</em>, invited merchants of all nations and religions to settle there, granting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> freedom of worship, protection and trading rights; Livorno became a cosmopolitan free port with one of the largest Jewish communities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and a hub of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> trade. The plague of 1630–1633 caused heavy mortality. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> was firmly upheld, and in the later seventeenth century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cosimo-iii-de-medici">Cosimo III</a> imposed strict religious and moral controls. In the eighteenth century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-ii-holy-roman-emperor">Peter Leopold</a> freed the grain trade in 1767, abolished the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> in 1770, began draining the marshes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/val-di-chiana">Val di Chiana</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maremma">Maremma</a>, and sought to create a class of small landowners, making Tuscany a laboratory of physiocratic economic reform.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lepanto"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-lepanto.jpg" alt="Battle of Lepanto" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lepanto">Battle of Lepanto</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Laureys_a_Castro_-_The_Battle_of_Lepanto.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Laureys a Castro, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Tuscany was a small state that relied more on diplomacy and fortification than on armies. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cosimo-i-de-medici">Cosimo I</a>’s wars against the Florentine exiles and Siena were his main campaigns, and he built a militia and fortresses throughout the state. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> was overlooked by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fortezza-da-basso">Fortezza da Basso</a>, begun under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alessandro-de-medici">Alessandro</a> in 1534, and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/forte-di-belvedere">Forte di Belvedere</a>, designed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernardo-buontalenti">Bernardo Buontalenti</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinando-i-de-medici">Ferdinando I</a> in the 1590s. On <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elba">Elba</a> Cosimo founded the fortified port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portoferraio">Portoferraio</a>, originally named Cosmopoli, in 1548, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livorno">Livorno</a> was enclosed by modern bastions. At sea, Cosimo founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-saint-stephen-tuscany">Order of Saint Stephen</a> in 1561, a crusading order of knights based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a>, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a> fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbary-coast">Barbary</a> corsairs; Tuscan galleys served in the Christian fleet at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lepanto">Battle of Lepanto</a> in 1571, and in 1607 the knights sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annaba">Bona</a> on the Algerian coast. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinando-ii-de-medici">Ferdinando II</a> joined the league against the Barberini in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wars-of-castro">War of Castro</a> in the 1640s, but after the mid-seventeenth century the grand dukes avoided war, and the state was largely demilitarised, especially under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-ii-holy-roman-emperor">Peter Leopold</a>, who reduced the army sharply. Tuscany was therefore defenceless before the French in 1799. In 1848 Tuscan volunteers, many of them students from Pisa and Siena, fought the Austrians at Curtatone and Montanara.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/cosimo-i-de-medici"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cosimo-i-de-medici.jpg" alt="Cosimo I de' Medici" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/cosimo-i-de-medici">Cosimo I de' Medici</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Agnolo_Bronzino_-_Cosimo_I_de%27_Medici_in_armour_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bronzino, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cosimo-i-de-medici">Cosimo I</a> transformed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> from a republic into a centralised princely state. He kept some republican councils, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senate-of-forty-eight">Senate of Forty-Eight</a>, but real authority lay with the grand duke, his secretaries and a reorganised set of magistracies, for which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giorgio-vasari">Giorgio Vasari</a> built the Uffizi, literally ‘the offices’, from 1560. Siena kept its own laws and administration under a governor. The Medici grand dukes ruled as absolute monarchs: Francesco I (r. 1574–1587), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinando-i-de-medici">Ferdinando I</a> (r. 1587–1609), a former cardinal and able administrator, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cosimo-ii-de-medici">Cosimo II</a> (r. 1609–1621), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinando-ii-de-medici">Ferdinando II</a> (r. 1621–1670), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cosimo-iii-de-medici">Cosimo III</a> (r. 1670–1723) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gian-gastone-de-medici">Gian Gastone</a> (r. 1723–1737). As the family faced extinction, the great powers decided the succession, and under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-vienna-1738">Treaty of Vienna</a> Tuscany passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i-holy-roman-emperor">Francis Stephen of Lorraine</a>, husband of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a>, who governed it through a regency council in Florence. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-ii-holy-roman-emperor">Peter Leopold</a> (r. 1765–1790) was one of the most consistent reformers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a>. He reorganised local government, abolished the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-inquisition">Inquisition</a> in Tuscany, reformed taxation and debated a written constitution that was never enacted. His criminal code of 30 November 1786, the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopoldina">Leopoldina</a></em>, inspired by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesare-beccaria">Cesare Beccaria</a>, abolished torture, the crime of lèse-majesté and the death penalty, and is often described as the first modern legal code to abolish capital punishment.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pitti-palace"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pitti-palace.jpg" alt="Pitti Palace" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pitti-palace">Pitti Palace</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Palazzo_Pitti_nel_tardo_pomeriggio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Almaak, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Medici Tuscany remained one of the great centres of European culture after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galileo-galilei">Galileo Galilei</a> named the moons of Jupiter that he discovered in 1610 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilean-moons">Medicean Stars</a> in honour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cosimo-ii-de-medici">Cosimo II</a>, who then appointed him court mathematician and philosopher; he remained under Medici protection, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcetri">Arcetri</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, after his condemnation by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-inquisition">Inquisition</a> in 1633. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/accademia-del-cimento">Accademia del Cimento</a>, founded in 1657 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinando-ii-de-medici">Ferdinando II</a> and his brother Leopoldo de’ Medici, was one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>’s first scientific societies devoted to experiment. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/accademia-della-crusca">Accademia della Crusca</a>, founded in the early 1580s, published its <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vocabolario-degli-accademici-della-crusca">Vocabolario</a></em> in 1612, making Florentine the model for literary Italian. Opera was born in Florence: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florentine-camerata">Florentine Camerata</a> explored new styles, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacopo-peri">Jacopo Peri</a>’s <em>Euridice</em> was performed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pitti-palace">Pitti Palace</a> in 1600 for the wedding of Maria de’ Medici and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iv-of-france">Henry IV of France</a>. The grand dukes enlarged the Pitti Palace and laid out the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boboli-gardens">Boboli Gardens</a>, and their collections became the Uffizi Gallery. In 1737 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anna-maria-luisa-de-medici">Anna Maria Luisa</a> de’ Medici, the last of the line, bequeathed the family’s art to the state on condition that it never leave Florence, a decision that preserved the city’s treasures. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-ii-holy-roman-emperor">Peter Leopold</a>’s abolition of the death penalty is commemorated each 30 November as the Festa della Toscana.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-luneville"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-luneville.jpg" alt="Treaty of Lunéville" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-luneville">Treaty of Lunéville</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Strategic_Situation_of_Europe_1801.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Medici state lost economic vitality in the seventeenth century, and the extinction of the dynasty in 1737 left Tuscany’s fate to the great powers. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a> reforms revived it, but it could not resist revolutionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. French troops occupied Tuscany in 1799, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-luneville">Treaty of Lunéville</a> it was turned into the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruria">Etruria</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-i-of-etruria">Louis of Bourbon-Parma</a> in 1801. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> annexed it to France in 1807–1808, and from 1809 his sister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elisa-bonaparte">Elisa Bonaparte</a> governed it as grand duchess. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-iii-grand-duke-of-tuscany">Ferdinand III</a> was restored in 1814, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> confirmed the grand duchy, which absorbed the Duchy of Lucca in 1847. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-ii-grand-duke-of-tuscany">Leopold II</a> (r. 1824–1859) was a relatively mild ruler who drained marshes and tolerated a degree of intellectual freedom, but in 1848 he granted a constitution, then fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaeta">Gaeta</a> in 1849 as democrats took power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, and returned with Austrian troops, which stayed until 1855. When war broke out between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and France with Piedmont-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> in 1859, Florentines demonstrated for the national cause, and on 27 April Leopold left Tuscany for good. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bettino-ricasoli">Bettino Ricasoli</a> the provisional government joined the United Provinces of Central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and a plebiscite in March 1860 approved annexation to the Kingdom of Sardinia; Florence was capital of Italy from 1865 to 1871.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Grand Duchy of Tuscany, created for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cosimo-i-de-medici">Cosimo I</a> de’ Medici in 1569, sustained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> as a centre of science, language and the arts under the Medici, sheltered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galileo-galilei">Galileo</a> and saw the birth of opera, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-ii-holy-roman-emperor">Peter Leopold of Habsburg-Lorraine</a> was often described as the first modern state to abolish the death penalty.</em></p>

